1. Your Role and Responsibilities This Month (+ Evidence)
During Month 1, I joined PwC's Advisory – Microsoft Technology Consulting team as an Intern/Trainee based in the Amman office. My role focused on building foundational knowledge in the tools and platforms the team uses in client consulting projects, and applying that knowledge through hands-on practice projects.
Weeks 1–2: Learning the Core Platform
I started by studying the fundamentals of Microsoft Dynamics 365 and Power Platform — the main technology stack used by the team to deliver business solutions for clients. My work included:

Understanding how business applications are structured: data tables, forms, views, workflows, and security settings
Learning how to extend the platform using custom server-side code (plugins) to automate business logic
Creating internal documentation and study guides to consolidate my learning and share with the team

Weeks 2–3: Hands-On Plugin Development
I moved from theory to practice by working on plugin development tasks assigned by the team:

Built custom automation logic for a sample entity to auto-generate unique ID fields
Practiced error handling, debugging techniques, and testing approaches
Documented my learnings into structured guides covering 10 key competency areas for future reference

Weeks 3–4: Framework Study & Building Practice Projects
I expanded my scope to study PwC's proprietary Hexa BPM Framework — the tool the team uses to deliver process automation solutions for clients:

Studied the Hexa documentation end-to-end, covering how business processes, workflows, approvals, and business rules are configured
Created a series of summary documents explaining how Hexa works and how it compares to standard platform capabilities
Built a complete Visitor Management System as a practice project — including a business app, relational data tables, approval workflows, a dashboard, and role-based security
Started designing a Vendor Compliance & Certification System — a more advanced practice project inspired by Hexa's architecture

Upskilling Sessions & Team Engagement

Attended weekly Power Platform & Dynamics CRM Upskilling sessions with the team:

April 22: Business Apps & Canvas Apps Basics
April 29: Plugins, Workflows & Web Resources


Participated in Interns Bi-Weekly Checkpoint meetings (April 15 & 29) for progress tracking
Attended a team Catchup meeting on April 26


2. Any Problems / Challenges and How Did You Overcome Them?

Steep learning curve — The technology stack was new to me. Overcame by following a structured self-study approach: read → practice → document → repeat.
Understanding a proprietary framework without a live environment — Hexa is PwC's own tool, so public resources don't exist. Resolved by thoroughly studying internal documentation and building a similar practice project using standard tools to simulate its patterns.
Bridging theory and practice — Upskilling sessions covered concepts, but real understanding came from building projects independently. Resolved by designing and implementing two full practice projects end-to-end.
Debugging in a cloud-based environment — Unlike traditional development, cloud platform code can't be tested locally the same way. Overcame by learning the platform's built-in logging and debugging tools.


